(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-instrument-repair-decision :authorize-calibration-operation :proceed-with-calibration-procedure :override-safety-officer-judgment :override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007222-cloud-itonami-isco-7222-toolmakers-coverage" "2799007223-cloud-itonami-isco-7223-metal-working-machine-tool-setters-coverage" "2799007224-cloud-itonami-isco-7224-metal-polishers-wheel-grinders-tool-sharpeners-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the instrument-repair operation" "proceed with the instrument repair operation" "proceed with the calibration procedure" "proceed with the calibration work" "finalize the instrument-repair decision" "finalize the calibration decision" "authorize the instrument-repair operation" "authorize the calibration operation" "override the workshop safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override workshop safety officer judgment" "override safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007311), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :manufacturing/precision-instrument-workshop-scheduling-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :worker-verified :workshop-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7311"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007311: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7311 Precision-instrument Makers and Repairers workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7311\n-- Precision-instrument Makers and Repairers** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7311\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7311\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Precision-instrument Makers and\nRepairers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7311` (`gh\napi` 404 confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `7311` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nPrecision-instrument Makers and Repairers make and repair precision\ninstruments (scientific, optical, medical, navigational and\nwatch/clock instruments etc.) with fine tools and small\nmechanical/optical components on an active workshop bench -- real\nphysical-safety stakes (eye strain/hand injury from fine-detail work,\noccasional solder/chemical exposure). The controlling constraint for\nthis vertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any\nop that directly finalizes an instrument-repair/calibration-execution\ndecision, or overrides a workshop safety officer's judgment -- those\ndecisions must always be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate\nop, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to\nWORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\ninstrument-repair or calibration work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7222`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7222)\n(Toolmakers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph, full doc set, same Wave3 rollout, same precision-workshop\nphysical-safety-domain shape) -- cloned and read in full in this\nsession. The closed allowlist + always-escalate safety flag +\ncontent-based scope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test was\nmirrored closely from that same reference.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7311` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for precision-instrument\nmakers and repairers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture\nas every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Instrument\nMaker Advisor ⊣ InstrumentMakerGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct instrument-repair/calibration-\nexecution authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise,\nsimulate exercising, or propose exercising an instrument-repair/\ncalibration-execution decision, or any override of a workshop safety\nofficer's judgment. These are not merely gated behind escalation --\nthey are structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a tool-condition/eye-hazard/\n  chemical-exposure concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- precision-parts/materials procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** an\ninstrument-repair/calibration-execution decision or a\nworkshop-safety-officer-judgment override. These are **structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind\nescalation -- an op such as `:finalize-instrument-repair-decision`\ncannot even be proposed by the reference mock advisor (its\n`infer`/`rationale-for` functions only ever emit one of the four\nallowlist ops), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM\nadvisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such an op, the governor's\nclosed-allowlist HARD check (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally\nand permanently. A second, independent HARD check\n(`:scope-excluded-action`) names five concretely-forbidden ops\n(`instrumentmaker.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-instrument-repair-decision`,\n`:authorize-calibration-operation`,\n`:proceed-with-calibration-procedure`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and\nis verified by dedicated unit tests\n(`instrumentmaker.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-instrument-repair-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-calibration-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-calibration-procedure`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered worker record (`:no-worker`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the worker is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop (`:no-workshop`). Together with\n   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a\n   worker/workshop record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with an\n   instrument-repair or calibration procedure, or overriding a\n   workshop safety officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT\n   block (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on\n   content -- defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if\n   a future change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based\n   rejection still independently blocks instrument-repair/\n   calibration-execution/safety-override language wherever it appears\n   in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an eye-strain/\n  hand-injury/solder- or chemical-exposure observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about\n  workshop safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  precision-parts/materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike an\n  instrument-repair/calibration-execution or safety-officer-override\n  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft\n  escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"instrument\", \"calibration\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside\nthe mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly\nthe risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a workshop coordination actor\nworking adjacent to instrument-repair/calibration work necessarily\ndiscusses those concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could\ntrigger if phrased carelessly. `instrumentmaker.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`\nis phrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g.\n\"proceed with the calibration procedure\", \"authorize the\ninstrument-repair operation\", \"override the workshop safety officer's\njudgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example phrasing), never\nbare nouns, and\n`instrumentmaker.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for worker ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for\ninstrument-repair task ...\", \"... routed for workshop safety officer\nreview\") WITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action\nphrases, so the regression test is a genuine stress test of the\nbare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on\nfirst run in this session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard\nitself was never tripped during development.\n`instrumentmaker.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7311\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7311` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7311`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 47 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-instrument-repair-decision` or\n  `:approve-calibration-procedure` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to finalize an instrument-repair/calibration-execution\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision this severe (fine-detail-work hazard,\n  solder/chemical exposure), the capability itself must not exist. The\n  only op related to workshop-safety escalation is\n  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human/workshop-safety-officer attention and never itself decides,\n  authorizes, or defers an instrument-repair/calibration decision for\n  later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring earlier\n  reference actors' transaction-amount-ceiling HARD checks): rejected\n  per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold\n  precision-parts/materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is\n  handled as a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the\n  instrument-repair/calibration-execution/safety-officer-override\n  class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"instrument\"\n  \"calibration\" \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked\n  example for this exact task), and per the explicit instruction to\n  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,\n  never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  instrument/calibration/workshop-safety-officer concepts to do its\n  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking\n  the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly\n  against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare\n  nouns).\n- Merging `worker` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered worker AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered worker AND\n   workshop (`:no-worker`/`:no-workshop`). Not an instrument-repair/\n   calibration-execution or safety-officer decision in any respect --\n   it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry an instrument-repair/calibration-execution\n   decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such\n   attempt via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action`\n   content check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any\n   conclusion about a specific instrument-repair or calibration step\n   -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an eye-strain/hand-injury/\n   solder- or chemical-exposure concern to a human/workshop safety\n   officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence\n   level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through\n   which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the workshop\n   safety officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- precision-parts/materials\n   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not an instrument-repair/\n   calibration-execution or authorization decision of any kind -- it\n   is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\nan instrument-repair/calibration-execution decision (e.g. proceeding\nwith a specific instrument-repair or calibration procedure) or a\nworkshop-safety-officer-judgment override -- these categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`instrumentmaker.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five\nconcretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit\nlevel (`instrumentmaker.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`instrumentmaker.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-instrument-repair-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-calibration-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-calibration-procedure`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the\n`:op` field (`instrumentmaker.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with a calibration procedure or override a\nworkshop safety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted\nop\n(`instrumentmaker.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-workshop-safety-officer`).\nThis actor coordinates WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it never\nperforms instrument-repair or calibration work itself.\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 22, :assertions 47, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Precision-instrument Workshop Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :worker-verification :workshop-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7311"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7311"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7311)), (:test/simulator :instrumentmaker.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7311 — Precision-instrument Makers and Repairers Workshop Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7311)))
